    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_e92458627d34b6996274522876d609ef3435dae1b4e6b3ddb4e410910738fa2d",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Finite computation can certify a universal statement below a fixed height without constraining the unbounded tail. Numerical persistence is especially weak when slow divergence mimics boundedness; for the cited finite-prime approximants, a genuine global inference would instead require locally uniform holomorphic convergence and a zero-stability step such as Hurwitz.",
